-- title:
--   Ideal sheaf of a closed immersion Spec K → C
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $C$ a scheme, and $\sigma \colon \operatorname{Spec} K \to C$ a morphism of schemes which is a closed immersion; write $x = \sigma(\mathrm{pt})$ for the image under the underlying continuous map $\sigma$ of the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} K$. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements about the kernel ideal sheaf $\sigma.\mathrm{ker} \subseteq \mathcal O_C$ (Mathlib's `Scheme.Hom.ker`, the quasi-coherent ideal sheaf of the morphism). First, the support of $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$, as a subset of the underlying topological space of $C$, is the singleton $\{x\}$. Second, for every affine open $U$ of $C$ (an element of `C.affineOpens`) and every proof that $x \in U$, the image of the ideal $(\sigma.\mathrm{ker})(U) \subseteq \mathcal O_C(U)$ under the germ map $\mathcal O_C(U) \to \mathcal O_{C,x}$ — that is, the ideal of $\mathcal O_{C,x}$ generated by that image — equals the maximal ideal of the local ring $\mathcal O_{C,x}$.
--
--   This identifies the ideal sheaf of a $K$-rational closed point of a scheme: it is concentrated at the point, and its germ there generates the maximal ideal of the local ring. It is used in the analysis of the ideal sheaves of points on models of the modular curve $X_1(p)$, where the sections cutting out a rational point must be compared with the maximal ideal at that point.
-- source:
